# gnome-shell -- the desktop this whole chain was built to reach.
#
# GNOME 48 series, BLFS 12.4, which pins 48.4. The book was checked rather than
# the download directory (48.8 exists upstream), and its mutter pin -- 48.4 --
# matches the mutter already published here, which is the control that says the
# right edition of the book is being read.
SRC_URL=https://download.gnome.org/sources/gnome-shell/48/gnome-shell-48.4.tar.xz
SRC_SHA256=40e2ed74b45367f0ca38fbfaa0ab470868d23591d03dc40d0abd6ff77d23b707
SRC_DIR=gnome-shell-48.4

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# -Dsystemd=false, AND THE REASON IS NOT "THERE IS NO SYSTEMD"
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# meson.build:126-133 makes TWO lookups inside one `if get_option('systemd')`:
#
#     libsystemd_dep = dependency('libsystemd', version: '>= 246')
#     systemd_dep    = dependency('systemd',    version: '>= 246')
#     systemduserunitdir = systemd_dep.get_variable('systemduserunitdir', ...)
#
# and they fail differently, which is the part worth knowing. elogind installs
# libelogind.pc AND a libsystemd.pc SYMLINK, but NOT systemd.pc -- established
# in this tree three times over, in NetworkManager, ModemManager and
# accountsservice's pkg.env. So the FIRST call SUCCEEDS against the wrong
# provider and the SECOND fails outright.
#
# Left at its default this does not degrade, it breaks the build -- and it would
# break on the second line, naming 'systemd', after the first line had already
# quietly bound the shell to elogind. Turning it off is the same call at-spi2-core
# records for -Duse_systemd, and BLFS 12.4 passes -D systemd=false here too.
#
# Nothing is lost that this tree can use: what the option gates is
# org.gnome.Shell@wayland.service and friends (data/meson.build:128-147), user
# units for a systemd user session that does not exist here.
#
# -Dtests=false: they default ON and this pin is LOAD-BEARING, not tidiness.
#
# The tests are gated on `get_option('tests') and have_x11_client`
# (meson.build:324), and it is tempting to read our Wayland-only mutter as
# closing that gate by itself. IT DOES NOT. mutter/meson.build:138 is
#
#     have_x11_client = have_x11 or have_xwayland
#
# and this tree's mutter is built -Dx11=false -Dxwayland=true, so
# have_x11_client is TRUE and the second half of the gate is open. The tests
# would build. BLFS passes this option too.
#
# This is also why dependency('x11') fires below: gnome-shell's meson.build:94
# takes it under `if have_x11_client or have_x11`, so libX11 is a real
# dependency of this package despite there being no X server in the session.
#
# -Dextensions_tool=true IS PINNED THOUGH IT IS THE DEFAULT, and that is
# deliberate. It is the sole reason gnome-autoar is packaged in this tree:
# subprojects/extensions-tool/meson.build:32 takes dependency('gnome-autoar-0')
# with no required:false. Anyone hitting a gnome-autoar problem later will reach
# for -Dextensions_tool=false as the fix; this line and gnome-autoar's pkg.env
# are what say that the tool is wanted and the library is its consequence.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LEFT AT DEFAULT, EACH WITH ITS DEPENDENCY CONFIRMED PRESENT RATHER THAN HOPED
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# All of these default TRUE, and a defaulted requirement is a silent one, so each
# was checked against the tree before being left alone:
#
#   extensions_app   subproject; needs gjs and, at runtime, libadwaita 1.7.2
#   man              meson.build:137-139 gates find_program('rst2man') behind the
#                    option and only then does subdir('man') -- unlike mutter,
#                    whose subdir was unconditional and cost a CI round.
#                    python-docutils 0.21.2 supplies rst2man.
#   networkmanager   libnm from NetworkManager 1.54 and libsecret 0.21.7
#   portal_helper    meson.build:114-117 raises error() if it is on without
#                    networkmanager, so the two are kept consistent
#   camera_monitor   libpipewire-0.3 from pipewire 1.4.7, floor 0.3.49
#
# SASSC IS NOT REQUIRED, despite data/theme/meson.build:52 calling
# find_program('sassc'). The call sits inside `if not fs.exists(stylesheet)`, and
# all three stylesheets ship PRE-BUILT in the release tarball -- it is a path for
# git builds. Recorded because reading that call without its guard would have
# added a build dependency this package does not have.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE SECOND CROSS-PACKAGE VARIABLE, CHECKED RATHER THAN ASSUMED
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# meson.build:98-100 is a hard error:
#
#     if not have_fonts
#       error('Shell requires mutter to be built with fonts rendering support')
#
# and have_fonts is mutter_dep.get_variable('have_fonts') -- decided in MUTTER's
# recipe, not this one, exactly like have_x11_client above. mutter's
# meson.options:222-226 declares fonts as a boolean defaulting TRUE and this
# tree's mutter does not override it, so mutter exports have_fonts=true
# (mutter/src/meson.build:1380) and this error cannot fire. Verified rather than
# assumed, because nothing in THIS file could tell me.
#
# -Dtests=false has a third consequence found the same way: src/st/meson.build
# :214 takes dependency(libmutter-test-16) under the pair
# get_option('tests') and have_x11_client. With have_x11_client TRUE, only
# the tests option keeps that
# lookup from running against a test library this tree's mutter may not install.
#
# This option set was re-scanned in full -- every error() and every dependency()
# in every meson.build including subprojects, with no pipe to head -- after a
# truncated scan of evolution-data-server cost a CI round. That redo is what
# found json-glib in extensions-tool, which a first pass stopping at the
# gnome-autoar line had missed.
MESON_ARGS="-Dsystemd=false -Dtests=false -Dextensions_tool=true"
